From b8fd30973c63a53e934467b703ad08bbf82f5e47 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:04:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 103/206] feat(shorebird): analyze_snapshot --dump_blobs

The four snapshot regions concatenated, which their help describes as being for
avoiding Mach-O/ELF differences. On our own snapshot: 504742 bytes, and slicing
it back at each region's declared length reproduces the ELF's bytes exactly with
nothing left over.

The lengths are the ones the VM knows, and that was measured before writing
anything rather than assumed. On their base.so the blob is 669622 bytes while
.rodata plus .text is 669760 -- the 138-byte difference is section alignment
padding. Using the ELF section sizes would have produced a blob that looks right
and is wrong.

Neither length is in Dart_SnapshotAnalyzerInformation, which is why this was
recorded as blocked. They were found instead in the headers the regions carry:
Snapshot::length() for data, and Image's own size field for instructions.

An independent cross-check fell out: the isolate instructions region measures
295248 bytes, which is exactly where the last function ends according to the
InstructionsTable walk that --shorebird does. Two unrelated sources agreeing.

The withheld flag count drops from four to three. The two dump_object_pool
flags need op_subgraph_hash; --disassemble remains reverted over the VM flag
name collision.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

